  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "V.P.Musthafa vs Food Inspector on 4 February, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Thursday, The 4Th Day Of February 2016/15Th Magha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 2379 of 2004 --------------------------------- Crl.A 373/2001 of I Addl.Sessions Court, Kozhikode St 46/1997 of J.F.C.M. - Ii, Perambra Revision Petitioner/1St Accused: ---------------------------------------------------- V.P.Musthafa, Aged 50 Years, S/O.Late Khader, Vengappuram, Mowenchery P.O. Kannur District. By Advs.Sri.C.Khalid Sri.T.P.Sajid (Thalassery) Respondent/Complainant & State: ---------------------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Perambra Circle, Hq Vatakara Perambra Grama Panchayat. 2. State, Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. R1 & R2 By Public Prosecutor, Shri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 04-02-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - V.P.Musthafa vs Food Inspector on 4 February, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/179000976/ 1 Crl.R.P. No.2379 of 2004 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 4thday of February 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 2.9.1996 at 11.00 a.m., PW1, the Food Inspector, purchased three packets of tapioca chips from the shop of the first accused. The second accused was the licensee of the said shop. After sampling as per rules, one of the samples was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P13 report stating that the sample contained coaltar dye tartarzine and therefore, the same was adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, the second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P16 report of the Central Food Laboratory would show that the sample showed the presence of tartarzine and therefore, it was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW3 were examined and Exts. P1 to P17 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 and DW2 were examined and Exts.D1 and D2 were marked for the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P16 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P16 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court V.P.Musthafa vs Food Inspector on 4 February, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/179000976/ 2 acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P16 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P16 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court V.P.Musthafa vs Food Inspector on 4 February, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/179000976/ 2 acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
